## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## whole_improve_loop  (v2 — one agent, its natural flow, minimal framing)
##
## `improvement_prompt` is THE AXIS — one determined improvement applied
## across a whole codebase (e.g. "split every file over 600 lines into
## cohesive units", "converge duplicated retry logic onto a shared helper",
## "make error handling use pattern Y"). This is a refactoring-CAMPAIGN
## engine, not a scanner.
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 axis-sweep (ADR-057) OVER-FRAMED the work: a rigid
## ~16-node graph (enumerate → next_item → transform → verify → review →
## commit → advance → re_enumerate …) that fought the agent's native
## productivity, and whose BLOCKING upfront `enumerate` (an exhaustive
## whole-repo scan before any work) timed out on a large repo. The mined
## signature of a PRODUCTIVE human session says to do the opposite (see
## docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the deficit is framing,
## not capability"; "once framed, a campaign runs itself". So v2 gives ONE
## capable agent a mission + STANDING AUTONOMY and lets it work in its
## natural flow — a living todo list, one verified commit per site, in
## stride — instead of a multi-node assembly line.
##
## GRAPH (see the workflow block):
##   campaign     — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools, whole-repo
##                  context). Mission = the AXIS + a standing-autonomy
##                  contract that reproduces the productive-session shape
##                  AGENT-SIDE: a LIVING todo list born from a brief
##                  exploration (never frozen upfront phases); the repeated
##                  unit locate → smallest change → build → test → COMMIT,
##                  ~a few edits per commit, validation BEFORE the commit;
##                  commit each site AS it finishes (never batch). git IS the
##                  durable state — a re-dispatch just re-runs `campaign`,
##                  which reads `git log` and continues. No worklist/cursor
##                  scratch file. Termination output (G2): {axis_complete,
##                  commits_this_pass, sites_remaining, …}.
##   verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic: reads the `verify-build` skill
##     + verify_run  and writes the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE
##                  <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC tool node
##                  re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code (no LLM
##                  judgment). This is the tight real-feedback loop AND the
##                  anti-Goodhart truth oracle: the agent can't self-certify.
##   gate         — deterministic compute: green AND axis_complete → converged
##                  → ship; else loop back to `campaign` (RED → with the
##                  failure log so the agent fixes what it broke; green but
##                  sites remain → another pass). No LLM, no shell.
##   mr_gate      — deterministic: open the optional PR (the series of
##     + finalize_mr per-pass commits) when open_mr, else finish.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign) is capped by
## `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships what is banked. `iterion validate`
## reports NO undeclared cycle. LANDS CONTINUOUSLY: the agent commits each
## site in stride, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has banked every
## committed site (git is the state).
##
## RIGHT-ARTIFACT (docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md): the campaign commits
## the UNCOMMITTED working tree AFTER its own build+test passes, staging
## untracked files (`git add -A`) so a change that ADDS files actually lands;
## the deterministic verify gate then re-checks the committed tree.
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): the AXIS + the repo define the work. No language /
## package-manager literal, and no iterion-specific target path, appears in
## any var default, command body, or schema — campaign / verify_build are
## adaptive agents that read whatever repo they are pointed at.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (ADR-057; git history has the design): enumerate,
## next_item, transform, the per-item cross-family review NODES
## (alt / reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt / review_gate — the campaign
## self-reviews via build/test + its own judgment, like the manual
## pattern), commit_item, advance, re_enumerate, and the worklist.json /
## cursor scratch STATE (git is the state now). The ADR-052 mono/dual review
## topology no longer applies to this bot (no reviewer nodes); it stays on
## the other review-loop bots.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY (no worklist/cursor any more — git is the state).
  ## The engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR} to ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch
  ## non-sandboxed and to the container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a
  ## sandbox (never hardcode ~/.iterion here — the -full image's pinned
  ## non-host User cannot write a host-owned path → EACCES). Keeping it OUT of
  ## the target worktree is the repo-agnostic rule: never pollute someone
  ## else's tree.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/whole-improve-loop"

  ## THE AXIS. The determined improvement to apply across the whole codebase,
  ## site by site — passed as improvement_prompt. Empty (default) = the
  ## campaign identifies and sweeps the single highest-value cross-cutting
  ## improvement it can concretely name for THIS repo. scope_notes adds
  ## free-form context; scope_globs restricts WHERE (below).
  improvement_prompt: string = ""
  scope_notes: string = ""
  ## Optional path-scope filter (focused runs). A comma/space-separated list
  ## of fnmatch globs matched against workspace-relative paths; empty
  ## (default) = the WHOLE workspace. It narrows WHERE the campaign applies
  ## the axis (the WHAT is the axis); passed as guidance to the agent.
  ## Example: --var scope_globs="pkg/runtime,pkg/store".
  scope_globs: string = ""

  ## BASELINE (G5 — docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md). Known
  ## pre-existing test failures / flaky tests the campaign must SKIP rather
  ## than burn budget proving they aren't its own. Empty (default) = the
  ## campaign establishes it once cheaply (a `git stash`-based check) then
  ## skips what predates its work. The dispatcher/operator can pre-fill it.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each pass is
  ## one campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping what is banked) when
  ## the axis is reported complete + the tree is green, or when this cap is
  ## hit. Sizes the declared continuation loop. Raise it (with the budget) for
  ## a big axis on a large repo; the agent commits in stride, so every pass
  ## banks real, landed work.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds the
  ## loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is N+1 —
  ## max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles (observed live,
  ## dogfood 019f3d4d-1aed).
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── PR finalization (opt-in) ───────────────────────────────
  ## When open_mr=true, after convergence finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD
  ## (the SERIES of per-pass commits) to the forge and opens a pull
  ## request (PR; merge request on GitLab). Off by default so a plain
  ## local run just leaves the commits on
  ## the operator's branch. The comment→PR trigger sets this true. This bot
  ## runs in a per-run git worktree (engine default `worktree: auto`), so the
  ## commits advance the worktree HEAD and finalize_mr pushes that branch.
  ## Requires a forge_token secret (or host-authenticated gh/glab).
  open_mr: bool = false
  ## Branch name to push + open the PR from. Empty = derive a stable name
  ## from the run (iterion/improve/<run-id>).
  mr_branch: string = ""
  ## Integration base the PR targets. Empty = the repo's default branch.
  mr_base: string = ""
  ## Optional back-link target: the source issue this run answers, as
  ## "native:<id>" (local board) or a forge issue URL. When set, finalize_mr
  ## posts the opened PR's URL back as a comment on it.
  source_issue_ref: string = ""

  ## ─── Plan phase (cross-model pair review) ─────────────────────
  ## Resolved at LAUNCH by pkg/reviewtopology from the run's credentials:
  ## auto → "on" iff a SECOND model family is credentialed (host
  ## detection locally, the sealed bundle on cloud), else "off" — the
  ## v2 plan-in-stride shape, at zero extra cost. "on" forces the phase
  ## (the peer reviewer then fails loudly on a missing credential).
  plan_review: string = "auto"
  ## What a MID-RUN peer failure (forfait window shut, provider down,
  ## credential revoked) does: "wait" (default) parks the run
  ## failed_resumable until the peer window reopens (run-level
  ## usage-window retry; cloud durable, local --auto-resume); "skip"
  ## arms the reviewer's `action: skip` fallback route — the plan
  ## proceeds unreviewed, LOUDLY (_skipped stamped, plan_gate reads it).
  plan_review_policy: string = "wait"

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + PR creation + the issue back-link. Mounted as a
  ## read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from; never read into a
  ## prompt. optional:true so manual/local runs relying on host-authenticated
  ## gh/glab still work. See skills/forge-mr-create.md.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior engineer running a focused improvement CAMPAIGN on ONE
  axis across a whole codebase — working EXACTLY as you would in a
  productive, human-driven engineering session. You have the full repo in
  front of you and full tools (read, edit, shell, git). No one micro-manages
  you; you own this pass end to end.

  THE AXIS is in your user prompt (`improvement_prompt`). It is the ONE
  determined improvement to apply everywhere it applies (e.g. "split every
  source file over 600 lines into cohesive units", "converge duplicated retry
  logic onto a shared helper", "make error handling use pattern Y"). If it is
  EMPTY, identify the single HIGHEST-VALUE cross-cutting improvement you can
  concretely name for THIS repo (a real, repeated, mechanical-enough pattern,
  not a vague "make it better"), state it in `summary`, and sweep THAT.

  HOW TO WORK — reproduce the shape of a productive session, agent-side:

  1. LIVING TODO LIST, not frozen phases. Start with a BRIEF exploration
     (grep/glob/read, or read-only sub-agents on a large repo) to find the
     concrete sites the axis touches, and build a todo list from what you
     find. Keep it DENSE and MOVING — re-prioritize, append, and defer items
     as you learn. Do NOT write 5–6 coarse upfront phases and tick them: the
     list is a metronome born from exploration, not a plan frozen before it.

  2. THE REPEATED UNIT, one site at a time: locate the exact site → make the
     SMALLEST change that applies the axis there → build → run the tests →
     COMMIT it with a semantic message (`refactor(improve): <precise site>`,
     or the `type(scope):` that fits — `fix(...)` for a bug axis, etc.). A few
     edits per commit, validation BEFORE the commit — never commit code you
     have not built and tested green.

  3. COMMIT EACH SITE AS YOU FINISH IT — never batch. Stage everything
     including new files (`git add -A`, so a change that ADDS files actually
     lands) and commit before moving on. git IS your durable state: an
     interrupted run keeps every site you committed, and a fresh pass just
     reads `git log` and continues where you left off. Do NOT keep a separate
     worklist / cursor file — the todo list lives in your working memory, the
     commits are the record.
     END EVERY PASS ON A CLEAN TREE: if you must stop mid-site, either
     finish it to committable or discard the in-progress edits — `git
     restore --staged --worktree -- <the paths you touched>` and
     delete any new files you created; never a blanket `git clean`
     (the workspace may be the operator's own checkout). The
     deterministic gate verifies the WORKING TREE, and a half-done
     site (an un-vendored dependency, a dangling import) reds the gate
     as noise.

  4. BASELINE — don't chase failures you didn't cause. Pre-existing test
     failures / known-flaky tests are in your user prompt (`baseline`). Do NOT
     try to fix or investigate those; if a test was already red before your
     change, SKIP it and note it. If the baseline is empty, do ONE cheap
     `git stash`-based check to establish which failures pre-date your work,
     then stop investigating them. Don't burn budget proving a failure isn't
     yours.

  5. TEACH-BACK ON AMBIGUITY; BLOCK ONLY ON A REAL HARD STOP. If the axis
     admits more than one materially different reading — and ALWAYS when
     you picked the axis yourself (empty `improvement_prompt`) — post a
     teach-back ONCE (first pass only): call `ask_user_async` with the
     axis RESTATED IN YOUR OWN WORDS, the sweep you intend, and the
     assumptions you are proceeding on — then KEEP WORKING under those
     stated assumptions. Never ask "did I understand correctly?" — a
     yes/no invites a rubber stamp; a restatement can be corrected. If an
     answer lands in your message queue, fold it in; if none does, your
     assumptions stand and MUST reappear in `summary`. Reserve the
     BLOCKING `ask_user` for a genuine hard stop — a destructive
     trade-off, a policy call that could invalidate the whole sweep. Keep
     both RARE: a clear axis gets zero questions.

  KEEP GOING, one site at a time, until this pass has applied the axis
  everywhere you can within your budget. Then STOP and report.

  BEFORE YOU REPORT, sharpen the diff with three questions (advisory — the
  build/test gate still decides; this only improves what you ship, it never
  blocks it):
    - Fit: does each change serve the real intent of the axis end-to-end, or
      did you satisfy its letter while the underlying problem persists? A
      change that passes the gate but doesn't move the actual need is a
      façade — redo it, don't report it done.
    - Rot: did you leave duplication (a third helper doing what two siblings
      already do), a one-caller abstraction, or two ways to do the same thing?
      Reuse the sibling and collapse it now, within the footprint this pass
      already touched — leave the tree more coherent than you found it, not
      more tangled, and don't widen the pass into code the axis never reached.
    - Ratchet: for a site you had to fix, what CHEAP deterministic check would
      have caught it — a test or an assertion AT THAT SITE, landed in a target
      the repo's own build already runs? If one is that small, land it: in the
      same commit while you are still on that site, otherwise as its own
      follow-up commit. NEVER amend or rebase a commit you already landed —
      banked commits are the run's durable state and may already be pushed.
      A check that can fail on code THIS PASS DID NOT TOUCH (a new lint rule,
      a stricter build-time constraint, any repo-wide gate) is not local: it
      turns the deterministic gate red on work that isn't yours. Name it in
      your summary, never land it — and the same for one that is simply too
      big. A check you declined is not itself a remaining site: keep it out of
      sites_remaining. The exception to `never land it` is an axis that IS
      about such checks — then landing one IS the work, but land it only
      TOGETHER with the fixes it demands, and scope it so the tree it must
      green is one this run can green. If they don't all fit this pass, don't
      land the gate yet: that missing check is a genuine remaining site, so
      report it in sites_remaining and land it on the pass that can green the
      tree with it. This question sharpens what you ship; it never grows the
      pass, never blocks it.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the engine
  detects completion; an under-specified end just gets re-poked):
    - axis_complete: true ONLY when a fresh re-scan finds NO remaining site
      where the axis still applies. false if sites remain (a later pass
      continues from your commits).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - sites_remaining: a concise note of what still needs the axis (empty when
      axis_complete).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator decision.
    - summary: what you changed and how it applies the axis.

  Be honest. A DETERMINISTIC build/test gate re-checks the tree after you and
  the run loops you back for another pass until you report axis_complete AND
  the tree is green — so under-reporting only costs a pass, and over-reporting
  lands you right back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  THE IMPROVEMENT AXIS (empty = pick the single highest-value cross-cutting
  improvement you can concretely name for this repo, and state it in summary):
  {{vars.improvement_prompt}}

  Extra context / notes:
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  Path scope (empty = the whole workspace; otherwise only apply the axis under
  these globs):
  {{vars.scope_globs}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures / known-flaky tests to SKIP (empty =
  establish it once cheaply, then skip what predates your work):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the first pass;
  when present, the build/test tree was RED after your last pass — FIX exactly
  what this shows before continuing the axis):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  PEER-REVIEWED SWEEP PLAN (empty = no plan phase ran, plan in stride as
  usual; on continuation passes read `git log` instead). The plan is a
  MAP, not a contract — reality wins; note where you diverge:
  {{input.plan}}

  Peer critique of that plan (cross-model reviewer; the plan author
  already challenged it and integrated what held — weigh what remains
  on its merits, empty = none or the review was skipped):
  {{input.plan_critique}}

  Plan author's responses to the critique (accepted/rejected and why):
  {{input.plan_responses}}

  Work the axis one site at a time — locate → smallest change → build → test →
  commit — until this pass has applied it everywhere you can. Read `git log`
  first to see what earlier passes already landed. Then report the termination
  contract (axis_complete, commits_this_pass, sites_remaining).

## ─── verify (deterministic build/test gate, stack-agnostic) ─────────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build skill and writes
## the repo's real build+test into <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC
## tool node (verify_run) re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code. No
## language / package manager is named here — the gate stays universal while
## the verdict stays deterministic. verify_build does NOT fix code: a RED gate
## routes back to `campaign` (the one capable agent) to fix what it broke.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A campaign pass just applied changes across
  this repository and committed them. Your single job: capture how THIS repo
  builds and tests itself into a re-runnable script the deterministic gate
  will execute. You do NOT change code.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the `verify-build`
     skill. Use the repo's OWN toolchain (a task runner / Makefile target, a
     package-manager test script, or a language build+test command),
     HONOURING any pinned toolchain (version managers, lockfiles, devbox,
     GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path, NOT inside the repo — create it first with
     `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build AND its tests
     (or the closest fast equivalent) and exits non-zero on any failure. Keep
     it to the commands a developer of THIS repo would run; no network-heavy
     or destructive steps.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: some repos are already red on the base tree,
     before this run touched anything. Scope `verify.sh` to the build plus the
     test targets the campaign's changes actually affect (package/module
     level), so the gate measures THIS run's work and a red base can't absorb
     it — and list any excluded pre-existing failures in your summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid (the commands resolve).
     You do NOT fix code here: if the build is RED because the campaign broke
     something, the deterministic gate reports it and the campaign fixes it on
     the next pass. Just make `verify.sh` faithfully run the repo's real
     build+test.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config), write
     a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its real
  exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh` exists
  and reflects the repo's real build+test; summary = the command you settled
  on and any pre-existing failures you scoped out.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build + test into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  (an out-of-tree scratch path — see the system prompt + the `verify-build`
  skill). Do NOT change code; the deterministic gate runs your script and the
  campaign fixes any red on the next pass.

  MANDATORY (skill section 1b): if the repo's own CI enforces a
  codegen-drift gate (git diff --exit-code after a regen/vendor step, a
  drift-check task, test -z on git status --porcelain), verify.sh MUST
  mirror it — run the repo's regen command(s), then fail on drift. The
  deterministic precheck rejects a gateless verify.sh in that case
  (exit 3) and the loop burns a full pass re-learning this.

## ─── PR finalization prompts (opt-in) ────────────────────────────

prompt finalize_mr_system:
  You finalize a converged improvement run by opening ONE pull request
  (PR; merge request on GitLab) for the SERIES of per-pass commits this
  run produced, then (optionally)
  posting the PR link back to the source issue. You make NO further code
  changes.

  Read the `forge-mr-create` skill FIRST — it tells you how to detect the
  forge, authenticate from the mounted forge_token, push the branch, and open
  the PR (GitHub via gh, GitLab via glab, Forgejo/Gitea via REST).

  Procedure:
    1. Confirm there are commits to ship: the run's HEAD must be ahead of the
       base (`git rev-list --count origin/<base>..HEAD` > 0). If not, set
       opened=false with skipped_reason "no commits ahead of base" — do NOT
       open an empty PR.
    2. Determine the branch name (mr_branch if set, else a stable
       iterion/improve/<run-id> name) and the base (mr_base if set, else the
       repo's default branch — see the skill).
    3. Authenticate the matching forge CLI from the mounted forge_token file
       when present; otherwise assume host auth. If neither is available, set
       opened=false with a precise skipped_reason — never pretend.
    4. Push HEAD to the branch on the origin remote, then open the PR from
       that branch into the base, with a clear title + body summarising the
       improvement axis and the sites landed (use scope_notes for context).
    5. If source_issue_ref is set, post the opened PR URL back to it: a forge
       issue URL → comment via the same forge CLI; a "native:<id>" ref → use
       the board comment tool. Set back_linked accordingly.

  Return opened, url, branch, back_linked, skipped_reason and a short summary.
  Be honest: report exactly what happened.

prompt finalize_mr_user:
  Workspace: {{input.workspace_dir}}
  Desired branch (may be empty → derive): {{input.mr_branch}}
  Base (may be empty → repo default branch): {{input.mr_base}}
  Source issue to back-link (may be empty): {{input.source_issue_ref}}
  Context / scope: {{input.scope_notes}}

  Push the branch, open the PR, post the back-link when a source issue is
  given, and return the structured result.

## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign's input: the deterministic gate's failure log from the PREVIOUS
## pass (empty on the first pass and on a green-but-more-work continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  fail_log: string
  ## Plan-phase hand-off (empty when the phase is off / skipped / on
  ## continuation passes — git is the durable state there).
  plan: string
  plan_critique: string
  plan_responses: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output the engine
## keys convergence on. axis_complete is the done-oracle (a fresh re-scan
## finds nothing left); the rest is observability.
schema campaign_output:
  axis_complete: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  sites_remaining: string
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC gate's contract. `passed` is set from
## the real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT an
## LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was produced
## (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## verify_probe output — deterministic "is a usable verify.sh already prepared?"
## so verify_build (an LLM agent, ~$0.69/13k tok EVERY pass) is only paid when
## the script is missing/invalid or on the first pass. fresh=true reuses it.
## verify_probe input — the continuation-loop iteration, mapped on the
## campaign→verify_probe edge. Loop refs ({{loop.*}}) do NOT resolve inside a
## tool command (only input/vars/run do), so the iteration is passed in as node
## input via the edge data-mapping. Pass 1 = iteration 0 (always regenerate).
schema verify_probe_input:
  iteration: int

schema verify_probe_state:
  fresh: bool
  reason: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = the tree is
## green AND the campaign reported the axis fully applied. fail_log relays the
## real build failure to the campaign on a RED pass (empty when green).
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

schema mr_gate_output:
  open_mr: bool

## forge_auth_probe output — deterministic "can we push?" so finalize_mr (an
## LLM agent) is only ever entered when a push credential actually exists.
schema forge_auth_state:
  available: bool
  reason: string

schema finalize_mr_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  mr_branch: string
  mr_base: string
  source_issue_ref: string
  scope_notes: string

schema finalize_mr_output:
  opened: bool
  url: string
  branch: string
  back_linked: bool
  skipped_reason: string
  summary: string

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## ─── Plan phase (opt-in cross-model pair review) ────────────────────
## Active only when `plan_review` resolved "on" at launch. Shape:
## the plan AUTHOR (claude family, read-only) maps the sweep → a peer
## from the OTHER family (claw + gpt) critiques it against the actual
## repo → the SAME author session challenges the critique and integrates
## what holds → the campaign implements. ONE revision turn, no loop —
## the campaign stays the arbiter of reality (asymptote intact). When
## off, entry routes straight to `campaign`: the v2 shape, unchanged.

prompt plan_system:
  You are the implementation planner for a codebase-improvement
  campaign. You are READ-ONLY: explore the repository and produce a
  PLAN — change nothing.

  Explore briefly (the implementing agent re-verifies reality): the
  axis's concrete sites, the existing patterns each change should
  reuse, the order that banks value earliest. Then write the plan as a
  MAP, not a contract — the implementer may diverge where reality
  disagrees.

  Structured output:
    - plan: the sweep plan — the axis stated concretely, then the sites
      /site-families in order, each naming the paths it touches.
    - assumptions: the load-bearing assumptions the plan rests on.
    - risks: where the plan is most likely wrong, and what to check.

prompt plan_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  THE IMPROVEMENT AXIS to plan (empty = pick the single highest-value
  cross-cutting improvement you can concretely name for this repo, and
  plan THAT — state it explicitly in the plan):
  {{vars.improvement_prompt}}

  Extra context / notes:
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  Path scope (empty = the whole workspace):
  {{vars.scope_globs}}

prompt plan_review_system:
  You are a peer reviewer from a DIFFERENT model family, adversarially
  reviewing an improvement-sweep plan against the actual repository.
  Your value is INDEPENDENT judgment: verify the plan's claims in the
  code with your read-only tools; do not extend the plan's own
  reasoning.

  Hunt for: a wrong or missing assumption about the code; a site list
  that misses where the axis really lives (or sweeps where it should
  not); a change that builds a parallel abstraction where an existing
  seam fits; a test gap; a materially simpler shape; an understated
  risk.

  Structured output:
    - concerns: the defects you can GROUND in the repo (name the
      file/seam per claim). Empty when the plan holds — do not
      manufacture concerns to look useful.
    - suggestions: concrete improvements, most valuable first.
    - blocking: true ONLY for a defect that would make the swept
      changes wrong (not merely improvable).

prompt plan_review_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  THE IMPROVEMENT AXIS the plan sweeps (empty = the plan names it):
  {{vars.improvement_prompt}}

  THE PLAN under review:
  {{input.plan}}

  Its stated assumptions:
  {{input.assumptions}}

  Its stated risks:
  {{input.risks}}

prompt plan_revise_system:
  You authored the sweep plan earlier in this session. A peer reviewer
  from another model family has critiqued it. CHALLENGE the critique:
  verify each concern against the repository, integrate what holds,
  refuse what does not — with a stated reason. You revise the PLAN
  only; you change no code.

  Structured output:
    - plan: the final plan, revised where the critique held.
    - review_responses: one line per concern — accepted (and how the
      plan changed) or rejected (and why).

prompt plan_revise_user:
  Peer critique of your plan:
  {{input.concerns}}

  Peer suggestions:
  {{input.suggestions}}

  Blocking: {{input.blocking}}

  Your plan as reviewed:
  {{input.plan}}

schema plan_topology_state:
  do_plan: bool

schema plan_output:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string

schema plan_review_input:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string

schema plan_review_output:
  concerns: string
  suggestions: string
  blocking: bool

schema plan_gate_state:
  skipped: bool

schema plan_revise_input:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string
  concerns: string
  suggestions: string
  blocking: bool

schema plan_revised_output:
  plan: string
  review_responses: string

## plan_topology lifts the launch-resolved plan_review var into a bool
## so the entry edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM.
compute plan_topology:
  description: "Plan phase gate (launch-resolved)"
  output: plan_topology_state
  expr:
    do_plan: "vars.plan_review == 'on'"

## plan — the AUTHOR. Same family/model as the campaign (the peer is the
## cross-check). readonly is enforced on codex/pi only — on claude_code
## (bypassPermissions) it is intent, not a guarantee; the deterministic
## verify gate + the campaign's own review remain the backstop.
agent plan:
  description: "Author the sweep plan (read-only)"
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  output: plan_output
  system: plan_system
  user: plan_user
  session: fresh
  readonly: true

## plan_review — the PEER, pinned to the other family. claw because
## "openai/*" models only resolve there; both knobs env-overridable so a
## host without OpenAI quota can run the peer elsewhere. Read-only
## toolset (no write_file/file_edit tools — though bash can write; the
## restriction is intent + review-pr's shipped posture, not a sandbox).
judge plan_review:
  description: "Peer plan review (other model family)"
  backend: "${ITERION_PLAN_REVIEW_BACKEND_GPT:-claw}"
  model: "${ITERION_PLAN_REVIEW_MODEL_GPT:-openai/gpt-5.6-sol}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high}"
  input: plan_review_input
  output: plan_review_output
  system: plan_review_system
  user: plan_review_user
  session: fresh
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  tool_max_steps: 30
  fallbacks:
    ## The plan_review_policy switch, expressed as a route gate:
    ## policy "skip" arms this terminal route — a peer failure completes
    ## the node with a zero-value critique stamped _skipped (plan_gate
    ## routes around the revise turn). policy "wait" leaves NO active
    ## route: the failure stays failed_resumable and the run-level
    ## usage-window retry parks the run until the forfait reopens.
    give_up:
      action: skip
      when: "vars.plan_review_policy == 'skip'"
      on: [usage_window, unavailable, auth]

## plan_gate — did the peer actually serve, or did the skip route fire?
## Deterministic: reads the _skipped stamp, never an LLM judgment.
compute plan_gate:
  description: "Peer review served or skipped?"
  output: plan_gate_state
  expr:
    skipped: "outputs.plan_review._skipped == true"

## plan_revise — the AUTHOR again, SAME session (the `_session_id`
## carried on the edge), challenging the peer's critique. Read-only.
agent plan_revise:
  description: "Author challenges & integrates the critique"
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: plan_revise_input
  output: plan_revised_output
  system: plan_revise_system
  user: plan_revise_user
  session: inherit_if_available
  readonly: true

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset (read/edit/shell/git) so it works exactly like a native Claude
## Code session: a living todo list + one verified commit per site, in
## stride. session: fresh — a fresh pass re-derives its todo list from the
## repo + `git log` (git is the durable state). reasoning_effort high, not
## max: the productive-session data says the working SHAPE, not the model
## tier, is the lever (framing > capability). interaction: async — an
## ambiguous or self-picked axis gets a non-blocking teach-back
## (ask_user_async: restate + assumptions, keep working; answers land
## mid-run in the message queue) while the blocking ask_user stays for
## genuine hard stops (kept rare by the contract).
agent campaign:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: async

## verify_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-check: is a usable verify.sh already in the
## scratch dir from an earlier pass of THIS run? verify_build is an LLM agent
## (~$0.69/13k tok EVERY pass); once pass 1 has authored verify.sh, re-authoring
## it every pass is pure waste. This tool answers "reuse it?" in ~50ms of
## subprocess: fresh=true when verify.sh exists, is non-empty, and passes
## `sh -n` — AND this is not the first pass (iteration>0), so pass 1 of every run
## always (re)generates verify.sh for the CURRENT tree, defeating a stale script
## a prior run may have left in a persistent (non-sandbox) scratch dir. python3
## for safe JSON + no quote clashes (mirrors verify_run); read-only, idempotent.
tool verify_probe:
  command: `SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} ITER={{input.iteration}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
try:
    iteration = int(os.environ.get('ITER', '0') or '0')
except ValueError:
    iteration = 0
fresh = False
if iteration <= 0:
    reason = 'first pass of this run: (re)generate verify.sh for the current tree'
elif not os.path.isfile(script):
    reason = 'no verify.sh in the scratch dir yet'
elif os.path.getsize(script) == 0:
    reason = 'verify.sh is empty'
else:
    chk = subprocess.run(['sh', '-n', script], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)
    if chk.returncode != 0:
        reason = 'verify.sh failed the sh -n syntax check: ' + (chk.stdout or '')[-300:]
    else:
        fresh = True
        reason = 'verify.sh present and valid; reuse it (skip verify_build)'
print(json.dumps({'fresh': fresh, 'reason': reason}))
"`
  input: verify_probe_input
  output: verify_probe_state

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). fresh: whole-tree buildability, not a per-site
## session. high, not max: mechanical capture of the repo's build/test.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_VERIFY_BUILD:-medium}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. No language /
## package manager appears here — it executes whatever verify_build wrote, so
## the gate stays universal while the verdict stays deterministic. python3 for
## safe JSON + subprocess capture; double-quote-free body, no backticks (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
def tree_state():
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return set(l for l in p.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip())
    except Exception:
        return None
pre = tree_state()
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
# Deterministic codegen-drift tail (stack-agnostic; the gate stays
# deterministic per ADR-044). Only on a GREEN verify: (1) if the repo's own
# CI enforces a drift gate, verify.sh must mirror it; (2) a green verify
# must not leave new changes in the tree (regen output belongs in a commit).
if rc == 0:
    import glob
    def has_drift_gate(text):
        for l in text.splitlines():
            # git diff --exit-code is the canonical build-failing gate.
            if 'diff' in l and '--exit-code' in l:
                return True
            # --quiet / status --porcelain also appear as commit-if-changed
            # control flow in updater workflows (e.g. a Homebrew tap bump) --
            # only count them when the same line actually fails the build.
            gated = ('exit 1' in l) or ('|| exit' in l) or ('test -z' in l) or ('[ -z' in l)
            if gated and (('diff' in l and '--quiet' in l) or 'status --porcelain' in l):
                return True
        return False
    ci_hit = ''
    for pat in ('.github/workflows/*.yml', '.github/workflows/*.yaml', '.gitlab-ci.yml', '.circleci/config.yml'):
        for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ws, pat))):
            try:
                if has_drift_gate(open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()):
                    ci_hit = os.path.relpath(f, ws)
                    break
            except OSError:
                pass
        if ci_hit:
            break
    if ci_hit:
        try:
            vs = open(script, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
        except OSError:
            vs = ''
        if not has_drift_gate(vs):
            rc = 3
            out = out + chr(10) + 'DRIFT GATE MISSING: CI (' + ci_hit + ') enforces a codegen-drift gate but ' + script + ' has none -- edit the script per the verify-build skill section 1b (run the repo regen command, then: git diff --exit-code).'
    if rc == 0 and pre is not None:
        post = tree_state()
        if post is not None:
            fresh = sorted(post - pre)
            if fresh:
                rc = 4
                out = out + chr(10) + 'UNCOMMITTED REGEN OUTPUT: a green verify left new changes in the tree; commit (or gitignore) them:' + chr(10) + chr(10).join(fresh[:50])
try:
    with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. Reads the real gate result
## (outputs.verify_run.passed) AND the campaign's termination output
## (outputs.campaign.axis_complete), both fresh in the loop BODY (the same
## discipline the retired `advance` compute used to read the loop-head safely).
## converged = green AND the axis is fully applied. On a RED pass fail_log
## carries the real build failure to the campaign; empty when green. No LLM,
## no shell.
compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.campaign.axis_complete"
    fail_log: "if(outputs.verify_run.passed, '', outputs.verify_run.log_tail)"

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output field so the two outgoing
## edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM, no shell.
compute mr_gate:
  output: mr_gate_output
  expr:
    open_mr: "vars.open_mr"

## finalize_mr — opt-in PR creation shipping the SERIES of per-pass commits.
## claude_code so the bundled forge-mr-create skill is mirrored and the board
## comment tool (native back-link) is available.
## forge_auth_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-flight for the push. finalize_mr is an
## LLM agent (claude_code, opus); entering it with NO push credential mounted
## burns a whole turn just to REDISCOVER, in shell, that it can't push (~$0.26
## observed on branch-improve-loop run 019f415c — this node is byte-shared).
## This tool answers "is there a push credential?" in ~100ms of subprocess so
## the graph only pays finalize_mr when a push can actually happen. python3 for
## safe JSON + no quote clashes (mirrors verify_run); read-only, idempotent.
tool forge_auth_probe:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
tok = '/run/iterion/secrets/forge_token'
try:
    if os.path.isfile(tok) and os.path.getsize(tok) > 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'file:' + tok})); raise SystemExit(0)
except OSError:
    pass
for v in ('GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GITLAB_TOKEN', 'FORGEJO_TOKEN', 'GITEA_TOKEN'):
    if os.environ.get(v):
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'env:' + v})); raise SystemExit(0)
try:
    r = subprocess.run(['gh', 'auth', 'status'], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'gh host auth'})); raise SystemExit(0)
except Exception:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'available': False, 'reason': 'no forge_token secret, no *_TOKEN env, no gh host auth'}))
"`
  output: forge_auth_state

agent finalize_mr:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_WHOLE_IMPROVE_MR_MODEL:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_WHOLE_IMPROVE_MR_EFFORT:-medium}"
  input: finalize_mr_input
  output: finalize_mr_output
  system: finalize_mr_system
  user: finalize_mr_user
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  capabilities: [board.read, board.comment]
  ## 20 is ample for push + open-PR + back-link (~5-8 commands nominal); 40 was
  ## headroom for a degenerate credential-probe spin that forge_auth_probe now
  ## makes impossible (the node is only entered when a push credential exists).
  tool_max_steps: 20

## surface_pr_link — DETERMINISTIC result-link emitter for the studio run
## summary. finalize_mr is an LLM agent, but the studio surfaces a run's
## headline PR link from the deterministic `[iterion] preview_url=… kind=pr`
## directive a tool node prints on stdout — never from agent prose. Reached
## only when finalize_mr actually opened a PR (opened=true ⇒ url set); the
## python guard drops an empty url so the scanner never sees a malformed
## directive. read-only, idempotent.
schema surface_pr_link_input:
  url: string

tool surface_pr_link:
  input: surface_pr_link_input
  command: `URL={{input.url}} python3 -c "
import os
u = (os.environ.get('URL') or '').strip()
if u:
    print('[iterion] preview_url=' + u + ' kind=pr scope=external')
"`

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow whole_improve_loop:
  ## Entry is the plan-phase gate. When `plan_review` resolved OFF at
  ## launch it routes STRAIGHT to the campaign — the mission starts
  ## working immediately (no blocking upfront enumerate to time out on a
  ## large repo), exactly the v2 shape. When ON, the sweep plan is
  ## authored, peer-reviewed cross-model, and author-revised first. A
  ## re-dispatch's campaign reads `git log` and continues either way.
  entry: plan_topology

  ## No `sandbox:` block (ADR-082): the platform sandboxes by default
  ## (`sandbox: auto` — target repo's devcontainer, else the default
  ## image). The bot's own tools (gh/glab for the PR-comment tail) come
  ## from the sibling devbox.json; the target repo's toolchain from its
  ## own devcontainer/devbox.json.

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    ## +30m/+$15 headroom for the opt-in plan phase (ADR-091): when
    ## plan_review resolves on, ~3 LLM calls run before the campaign.
    max_duration: "2h30m"
    max_cost_usd: 75

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## Plan phase (skipped whole when plan_review resolved off).
  plan_topology -> plan when do_plan
  ## Every campaign_input field is mapped EXPLICITLY empty: an unmapped
  ## field is not "" but the raw {{input.x}} placeholder leaking into
  ## the prompt (the engine keeps unresolved refs as-is).
  plan_topology -> campaign when not do_plan with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: ""
  }

  plan -> plan_review with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}"
  }
  plan_review -> plan_gate
  ## Peer served → the plan AUTHOR (same session) challenges the
  ## critique and integrates what holds; skipped (policy skip + peer
  ## unavailable) → the unreviewed plan proceeds, loudly.
  plan_gate -> plan_revise when not skipped with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}",
    concerns:    "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    suggestions: "{{outputs.plan_review.suggestions}}",
    blocking:    "{{outputs.plan_review.blocking}}",
    _session_id: "{{outputs.plan._session_id}}",
    _session_fingerprint: "{{outputs.plan._session_fingerprint}}"
  }
  plan_gate -> campaign when skipped with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "", plan_responses: ""
  }
  plan_revise -> campaign with {
    fail_log:       "",
    plan:           "{{outputs.plan_revise.plan}}",
    plan_critique:  "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    plan_responses: "{{outputs.plan_revise.review_responses}}"
  }

  ## Each pass: the campaign applies the axis (committing each site in
  ## stride), then the deterministic build/test gate re-checks the tree.
  campaign -> verify_probe with {
    iteration: "{{loop.continuation_loop.iteration}}"
  }
  ## verify_probe reuses an already-valid verify.sh (skipping the LLM
  ## verify_build) on passes 2+; the first pass of every run regenerates it for
  ## the current tree. Both outgoing edges test the bool `fresh` (C012-exhaustive).
  verify_probe -> verify_run when fresh
  verify_probe -> verify_build when not fresh
  verify_build -> verify_run
  verify_run -> gate

  ## Converged (green AND the campaign reports the axis fully applied) → ship
  ## what is banked (optional PR, else finish).
  gate -> mr_gate when converged
  ## Not converged → another campaign pass. RED carries the failure log so the
  ## agent fixes what it broke; green-but-more-work carries an empty log so the
  ## agent simply continues the axis. This closes the single declared loop
  ## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign); the loop-back reads
  ## outputs.gate.fail_log (a BODY node — never the loop-head campaign's own
  ## output on its back-edge, which would read a loop-entry-frozen value).
  ## The back-edge BLANKS the plan-phase fields: forward-edge mappings
  ## re-apply on every re-entry (outputs persist), and a stale pass-1
  ## plan would re-anchor later passes that must read `git log` instead.
  gate -> campaign as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}",
    plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: ""
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while sites still remain) →
  ## ship what is banked anyway (every committed site is real, landed work).
  ## Unconditional fallthrough after the loop-back retries are spent.
  gate -> mr_gate

  ## Converged (or capped) → optional PR path, else finish. When open_mr, first
  ## the deterministic forge_auth_probe checks a push credential EXISTS before we
  ## pay the finalize_mr agent turn.
  mr_gate -> forge_auth_probe when open_mr
  mr_gate -> done when not open_mr

  ## Push credential present → finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD (the series of
  ## per-pass commits) + opens the PR and (when source_issue_ref is set)
  ## posts the URL back. Absent → finish cleanly; the commits are on the
  ## engine's persistent iterion/run/<name> storage branch for a manual push.
  forge_auth_probe -> finalize_mr when available with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.scope_notes}}"
  }
  forge_auth_probe -> done when not available
  ## PR opened → surface its URL as a headline result-link in the run summary
  ## (deterministic stdout directive → preview_url_available kind=pr), then
  ## finish. Nothing opened (empty diff / refused PR) → straight to done.
  finalize_mr -> surface_pr_link when opened with { url: "{{outputs.finalize_mr.url}}" }
  finalize_mr -> done when not opened
  surface_pr_link -> done
